.TH LP 4 "November 26, 2026"
.UC 2
.SH NAME
lp \- line printer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.ft B
.nf
/sys/conf/PC:
.ta .5i +\w'LP_MAXCOL  'u +\w'lp_printers  'u
	NLP	\fIlp_printers\fP	# parallel ports: at most 3
	LP_MAXCOL	\fI132\fP	# Maximum number of columns on line printers
.DT

major device number(s):
	raw: 12
minor device encoding:
	bit 01 specifies 64-character set (instead of 96-character set)
	bits 06 are reserved
	bits 030 specify the parallel port: <lp_unit> * 8
.fi
.ft R
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Lp
provides the interface to a printer on one of a PC's parallel ports.
The ports are found as the system starts, where the BIOS looks for
them and in its order: 0x3BC, 0x378 and 0x278.
The first found is
.IR lp0 ,
the next
.IR lp1 ,
and so on.
Each has interrupt request 7, but one at 0x278 has 5.
When it is opened or closed, a suitable number
of page ejects is generated.
Bytes written are printed.
.PP
Opening a port fails with EIO if its printer is off line, out of
paper or reports an error, which
.IR lpd (8)
waits on and tries again; with EBUSY if a network card has the
port's interrupt; and with ENXIO if the port is open already or
was not found.
.PP
The unit number of the printer is specified by the minor device
after removing the low 3 bits, which act as per-device parameters.
Currently only the lowest of the low three bits is interpreted:
if it is set, the device is treated as having a 64-character set,
rather than a full 96-character set.
In the resulting half-ASCII mode, lower case letters are turned
into upper case and certain characters are escaped according to
the following table:
.PP
.br
.ns
.TP 10
{
\o"-("
.br
.ns
.TP 10
}
\o"-)"
.br
.ns
.TP 10
\`
\o"-\'"
.br
.ns
.TP 10
|
\o"\-!"
.br
.ns
.TP 10
~
\o"\-^"
..
.PP
The driver correctly interprets
carriage returns, backspaces, tabs, and form feeds.
A carriage return is sent before a line feed or a form feed
when the carriage is not at the left, as a PC's printer wants.
Lines longer than the maximum page width are truncated.
The page width is specified via the LP_MAXCOL definition,
set to 132 in the PC configuration.
.SH FILES
.ta \w'/dev/MAKEDEV.local  'u
/dev/lp?
.br
/dev/MAKEDEV	script to create special files
.br
/dev/MAKEDEV.local	script to localize special files
.DT
.SH "SEE ALSO"
lpr(1),
printcap(5),
lpd(8)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
\fBlp%d at 0x%x irq %d\fR.  A port found as the system starts.
.SH BUGS
A port whose interrupt request is not the one given above
sends a character only when the clock looks, every few seconds.
